Planned UI controls

The UI should expose controls for, at minimum:

Structural
Enable/disable each structural check.
Minimum episode frames.
Maximum episode frames.
Required columns where appropriate.
Video
Enable/disable video checks.
Frame-count tolerance in frames.
Timestamp tolerance in seconds or frames.
Auto/manual expected resolution.
Blur threshold.
Dark threshold.
Bright threshold.
Maximum bad-frame ratio.
Frozen-video frame-difference epsilon.
Frozen-video action-difference epsilon.
Minimum frozen duration.
Multiview sync tolerance in frames.
Camera enable/disable selection based on detected camera_keys.
Reference-camera selection (Auto or a declared camera).
Optional camera-coverage check.
Actions
Enable/disable range check.
Smoothness threshold.
Excluded dimensions selected by feature name.
Gripper dimension selected from discovered names.
Gripper open/close threshold.
Gripper maximum toggles.
Gripper minimum hold frames.
State/action maximum lag.
State/action minimum correlation.
Alignment settings.
Token-budget settings.
Episode quality
Idle velocity epsilon.
Maximum idle ratio.
Maximum boundary idle seconds.
Duration MAD threshold.
Path-efficiency threshold.
Proprioceptive sync tolerance.
Dataset quality
Duplicate detection settings.
Task-balance minimum per task.
Diversity settings.

Only controls supported by the current engine should be exposed as active controls.

Important UI design principle: metadata-driven controls

The UI should not contain fixed assumptions such as:

4 cameras
16 action dimensions
FPS = 30
resolution = 240x320

Instead, the UI should first load dataset metadata and generate controls dynamically.

For example:

Detected cameras: 3

should produce exactly three camera rows.

Similarly, action controls should display the actual feature names discovered from the dataset.

Planned control registry

Recommended next engine-facing module:

src/lerobot_validator/control_registry.py

Purpose:

Define each check's UI-facing metadata without moving the actual check logic into the UI.

Conceptual entry:

CheckDefinition(
    id="video.frame_count",
    name="Video frame count",
    phase="video",
    severity="HARD",
    controls=[
        Control(
            id="tolerance_frames",
            type="integer",
            default=1,
            min=0,
            max=10,
            unit="frames",
        )
    ],
)

The registry should describe:

Stable check ID.
Display name.
Phase.
Severity.
Enabled-by-default state.
Description/help text.
User-editable controls.
Control type.
Units.
Valid range.
Whether the value is auto-detected or user-controlled.

The registry is a UI contract, not a replacement for the validator implementation.

